#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084117} :activity/id #uuid "35a2312a-8238-5672-8715-5b99766aa324" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048254 :topic/id #uuid "8c8c4388-1538-58d1-b482-6285767c2635" :topic/math-academy-id 703} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084448 :tutorial/content "A **permutation** is a selection of objects in which order matters. Two selections of the same items are counted as different selections if they are in a different order.\n\nFor example, suppose that we have $5$ books, but our bookshelf can only hold $3.$ We want to place $3$ books on the shelf and the rest will stay in the drawer. \n\nHow many permutations are there? In other words, how many ways can we place $3$ books on the shelf (in a particular order)?\n\nLet's think about all the choices we have as we fill up the bookshelf:\n\n- We can place any of our $5$ books in the first slot of the bookshelf.\n\n- Now that we've placed one book on the bookshelf, we have $4$ books remaining. We can place any of these $4$ remaining books in the second slot.\n\n- Now that we've placed two books on the bookshelf, we have $3$ books remaining. We can place any of these $3$ remaining books in the third slot.\n\nThe number of choices we have for each slot is shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{5 choices} & \\text{4 choices} & \\text{3 choices}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of arrangements, we can use the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n5\\times4\\times3 = 60.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0e27ded9-9ee6-527a-9381-e7f6d0396030" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3682 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1f34cb92-fb54-5dc9-8f17-9675bc62c341" :step/math-academy-id 20569 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084246}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083221 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083021 :question/id #uuid "258b1f90-1c19-5a19-b796-a7c1c894bf17" :question/math-academy-id "e-5673"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "060e80f0-44bd-5378-9253-999548115dd9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050268}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522750}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Permutations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"} :step/id #uuid "f972487c-b2d6-5f63-935f-fb91b47f99b7" :step/math-academy-id 20570 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084119}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084119 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083222 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083022 :question/id #uuid "5431e96c-87d1-517b-a565-9621a0b67913" :question/math-academy-id "e-5674"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "256d3a08-0ade-51e9-887b-67250d5fae21" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418414}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Permutations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"} :step/id #uuid "39129bcf-9379-546f-a458-1226fc31f8fc" :step/math-academy-id 20571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083223 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083023 :question/id #uuid "c72ffaba-7a68-5070-920d-fe241be5c797" :question/math-academy-id "e-5675"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7c35604-c9f9-5077-bddc-fe9446ac45e6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394641}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Permutation Given Using Alternate Notation"} :step/id #uuid "a131104f-d99f-5253-bcb9-6403049716e4" :step/math-academy-id 20573 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084246 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084471 :tutorial/content "In general, the number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n_{n}P_r = \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\n$$\n\nIn the previous example, we had $n=5$ books and $r=3$ slots in the bookshelf. Using the formula, the total number of permutations is computed as\n![](images/3d/3d53f73ea9e1a5d0966237c002f2d11075ee10d519bb6a2823b2ff0fea8c8e53.svg)\n\nWe got the same result as before!\n\n**Note:** We can write ${}_nP_r$ in a number of ways:\n\n$$\n{}_nP_r = {}^nP_r = P(n,r)\n$$\n\nAll of these mean the same thing." :tutorial/id #uuid "7e83bfaa-0308-5b07-a516-02b67001ac8c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4477 :tutorial/title "The General Formula for Permutations"} :step/id #uuid "5daedd62-435e-56b3-aa28-cfa9a1b10d0f" :step/math-academy-id 27561 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084247}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084247 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083311 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083111 :question/id #uuid "a18e8e9a-da60-5a42-840a-60f83137c6e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7457"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9c145aa-bcbf-54bd-ba88-93f10aeb00f0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049341}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522718}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Permutation"} :step/id #uuid "ccdc7daa-24d6-512d-8379-a36e4ffbe8a3" :step/math-academy-id 27676 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084120}}] :activity/title "Permutations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083409}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418417} :activity/id #uuid "f2ef9b69-01fa-553b-9a02-e77367cdec27" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048254 :topic/id #uuid "8c8c4388-1538-58d1-b482-6285767c2635" :topic/math-academy-id 703} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418415 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaed84db-333e-5211-84c5-2d24c1d90316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,016" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394622} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c1b16c-2ec4-5950-92f8-59f867978e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394623} {:answer/id #uuid "542940cc-ac17-5702-9c22-d185b7bb47c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394624} {:answer/id #uuid "2e70f827-6004-57e0-825e-f0e45fc164d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,344" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394625} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0a0e84-7492-5601-b7a4-02b8e70ea90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\,120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394623} :answer-field/id #uuid "72d9fa78-c6ea-5ff0-a6cb-0b7ec2cbcfc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4ef67e9d-56e9-502f-9f38-dd41f4d01e7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-84983" :question/problem "A bag contains $9$ different colored marbles. Jamie takes $4$ marbles from the bag, one at a time, and puts them in the order they were taken from the bag to create a sequence of colors. How many different sequences of colors are possible?" :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ colored marbles, so $n=9.$ We want to select $4$ of those marbles to make a sequence of colors, so we have $r=4.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to choose the marbles is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{9}{P}_{4} & =\\frac{9!}{(9-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =3\\,024.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4438f96a-bc01-54cd-950c-bb4c7070f017" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418416}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4519364a-a039-5b52-8828-103d442a03a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "990" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418408} {:answer/id #uuid "5456ddc8-8ae6-57cd-97e3-f1eaa26ffc00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418409} {:answer/id #uuid "862c4b09-9172-545f-97c9-7f21e79cd3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418410} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a2ab60-18b2-5943-9f8e-856a28f1c403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "55\\,440" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418411} {:answer/id #uuid "ea315232-2913-5a30-877a-c82272b5ac01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\,920" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418412}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418412} :answer-field/id #uuid "69ac052e-a374-5b6c-af86-f7bda6ec7c19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9a0bfa46-736f-571e-9fd1-af2e7c4186b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-84984" :question/problem "A soccer coach must choose a defensive lineup for each of the $4$ defensive positions from a roster of $11$ defenders. How many potential starting defensive lineups are there?" :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $11$ defenders, so $n=11.$ We want to select $4$ of those defenders to play in each of the $4$ positions, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different starting defensive lineups is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{11}{P}_{4} & =\\frac{11!}{(11-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{11!}{7!} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}}{\\cancel{7!}} \\\\  & =11\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =7\\,920.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ef1e5084-9458-5f6f-93ee-c68edf6f4933"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418417 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83a899a2-01e7-51e1-97a0-7fb5af02afe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080334} {:answer/id #uuid "8528a6a0-4362-5bbb-ae9b-35bf75859f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080335} {:answer/id #uuid "a666991e-236d-5425-b8b6-617ba978b1d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080336} {:answer/id #uuid "abdb717b-c681-53a2-99b2-ee2ee784d6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080337} {:answer/id #uuid "b3677559-31a9-5adb-8a4c-96bb86c3ad1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080338}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186080335} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c35d8bc-e437-5909-9318-fd54b2619f04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a164e34b-9201-592b-aca6-126a5f507b56" :question/math-academy-id "q-1788" :question/problem "Suppose there are $7$ people who enter a race. Assuming there are no ties, in how many ways can the gold, silver, and bronze medals be awarded?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $7$ people, so $n=7.$ We want to select $3$ of those people for gold, silver and bronze, so we have $r=3.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to award the medals is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{7!}{(7-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{\\cancel{4!}} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =210.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fd11f4d8-2ca8-5342-ad46-6e479321ae16" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418415}}] :activity/title "Permutations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418407}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549119} :activity/id #uuid "9b7dddb8-f5d3-50d8-8850-49a9edd54ed3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048254 :topic/id #uuid "8c8c4388-1538-58d1-b482-6285767c2635" :topic/math-academy-id 703} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354377 :question/a